From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winex11, explorer: framed Linux windows switch, go full
 screen and keep their desktop

After 0762 put Linux programs' windows into Wine frames (David 2026-10-02,
release k):

- Alt+Tab from one did not switch on Alt's release: the Tab came to the
  frame (its key grab) but the release went to the program, which has the X
  focus. From the Tab until Alt is released the frame grabs the keyboard
  (sg_embed_key).
- the switcher destroyed itself before activating the window chosen; it was
  explorer's thread's active window, so Wine activated that thread's window
  before it -- a frame, explorer's too -- and the frame came back above the
  window chosen; the next Alt+Tab chose the same window again. Now the
  window chosen is activated (and so in front) first, from Alt+Tab and
  Task View.
- framed windows were "Linux windows" to the virtual desktops: on every
  desktop, and switched to through the compositor. Only the hidden
  stand-ins are now; a frame is a window like any program's (its desktop,
  raised when chosen, in Task View by its place).
- Task View and Alt+Tab drew them black: what Wine drew of a frame is the
  frame and a black client. Their pictures come from the screen, taken
  while they are in front, and never from PrintWindow.
- full screen (YouTube's button, F11) did nothing: the program's
  _NET_WM_STATE request went to the root, where no window manager manages
  it now. A thread of winex11's on its own connection hears the requests
  (SubstructureNotify on the root), keeps _NET_WM_STATE on the window and
  tells the frame, which goes popup over its monitor and back after. The
  window is kept in NormalState (WM_STATE): GTK takes a window without it
  for withdrawn and asks nobody.
- the taskbar was over a full-screen frame: it gets out of the way of
  one as of any full-screen window.

Test: test/linuxembed-gate.sh (Alt+Tab there and back, full screen in and
out, WM_STATE, its own desktop, Task View's card; mutants ALTTAB_NO_GRAB,
SWITCHER_DESTROY_FIRST, EMBED_NO_FULLSCREEN,
EMBED_WITHDRAWN, FRAME_EVERYWHERE, LINUX_THUMB_BLACK).
